    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Understood. Following up on that, how do courts usually handle cases where a tenant claims the disruption was caused by some kind of unpredictable, chaotic, or external factor that wasn't explicitly named in the clause? Is there a standard for what counts as \"unforeseeable\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Courts generally interpret force majeure clauses narrowly, meaning the disruptive event must closely match the language provided in the contract. The standard for \"unforeseeable\" is high; courts look for events that were genuinely outside the parties' reasonable control and could not have been mitigated. If the external factor isn't listed and doesn't obviously fit the defined scope, the tenant's claim often fails unless the contract has a broad catch-all phrase like \"any other cause beyond the parties' reasonable control.\"",
      "source": "target",
